> The PANACEA project is organised around six complementary Work Packages (WP), described below. WP1 Stakeholder Engagement shapes the various analyses with a broad co-design process, to ensure that the assumptions, scenarios, and outputs across the projects are appropriate to the intended users. WP2 - WP5 are sector-specific projects aimed at developing new methods to bridge disciplines and thereby better understand risks and adaptation options. WP6 Impact Synthesis brings together these new estimates with existing research to develop tools and datasets that can better inform stakeholders and low- and lower-middle income country (LLMIC) policy makers.
> 
> WP1. Stakeholder Co-design
> OBJECTIVES: Ensure that stakeholder views are incorporated into the project-wide analysis, through scenario and question development, identification of outcomes of interest; and engagement around results and policy directions.
> 
> PANACEA is a stakeholder-oriented project, with frequent interaction with local stakeholders to shape the project analysis and outcomes. The primary stakeholders for PANACEA are national-level policy makers, including representatives from Ministries of Finance and environment agencies. These will form (1) country-specific and project-wide advisory groups, and the core of (2) stakeholder engagement workshops occurring regularly during the project (see Gantt Chart). Building on the PIs existing relationships, the stakeholders will include:
> UK agency representatives for His Majesty's Treasury (HMT), Office of Budget Responsibility (OBR), and the Department for Environment, Food & Rural Affairs (Defra).
> Nigeria representatives of National Space Research and Development Agency (NASRDA) and the Food and Nutrition Technical Assistance Project (FANTA).
> Representatives of their Brazilian Ministry of Finance and the Novo Brasil initiative.
> 
> METHODS: The project-wide advisory group will include representatives of the (three) country-specific advisory groups. Each of the four advisory groups will meet quarterly virtually, and in-person workshops will be convened twice for each country (the PI has organised two large workshops on improving climate risk assessments, leading to Rising et al. 2022 and Royal Society 2023). Key decisions points include:
> Project-Wide decisions: Identify climate scenarios and global economic baselines: The PI has used off-the-shelf Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs), Network for Greening the Financial System (NGFS) scenarios, and probabilistic Resources for the Future Socioeconomic Projections (RFF-SPs) in past work, but stakeholder engagement under a current project suggests that policy makers are more interested in "most likely" and "worst case" scenarios.
> Country-specific decisions: (1) Develop risk and adaptation storylines: Domestic and international storylines for each country will provide context and applications for the quantitative risk outcomes and adaptation models (WP2 - 5 and WP6). These will be developed through workshops. Meetings will also discuss the significance of results to active policy discussions. (2) Design interactive visualisations to inform decision-making: Interactive displays will allow stakeholders to explore risks across dimensions of region, time, scenario, and sector, and the benefits of adaptation, across adaptation solutions and investment plans. Workshops will be used to design and refine interfaces to the project dataset.
