> 
> The PANACEA project is organised around six complementary Work Packages (WP), described below. WP1 Stakeholder Engagement shapes the various analyses with a broad co-design process, ensuring that the assumptions, scenarios, and outputs across the projects are appropriate for the intended users. WP2 to WP5 are sector-specific projects aimed at developing innovative methods to bridge disciplines, thereby enhancing our understanding of risks and adaptation options. WP6 Impact Synthesis integrates these new estimates with existing research to develop tools and datasets that can better inform stakeholders and low- and lower-middle-income country (LLMIC) policymakers.
> 
> **WP1. Stakeholder Co-design**
> 
> **OBJECTIVES:** Ensure that stakeholder views are incorporated into the project-wide analysis through scenario and question development, identification of outcomes of interest, and engagement concerning results and policy directions.
> 
> PANACEA is a stakeholder-oriented project, characterised by frequent interaction with local stakeholders to shape the project analysis and outcomes. The primary stakeholders for PANACEA are national-level policymakers, including representatives from Ministries of Finance and environment agencies. These stakeholders will form (1) country-specific and project-wide advisory groups, and the core of (2) stakeholder engagement workshops conducted regularly throughout the project (see Gantt Chart). Building on the PIs' existing relationships, the stakeholders will include:
> - UK agency representatives from His Majesty's Treasury (HMT), the Office of Budget Responsibility (OBR), and the Department for Environment, Food & Rural Affairs (Defra).
> - Nigerian representatives from the National Space Research and Development Agency (NASRDA) and the Food and Nutrition Technical Assistance Project (FANTA).
> - Representatives from Brazil's Ministry of Finance and the Novo Brasil initiative.
> 
> **METHODS:** The project-wide advisory group will include representatives from the three country-specific advisory groups. Each of the four advisory groups will meet quarterly virtually, and in-person workshops will convene twice for each country (the PI has organised two large workshops on improving climate risk assessments, resulting in Rising et al. 2022 and Royal Society 2023). Key decision points include:
> 
> - **Project-Wide Decisions:** Identify climate scenarios and global economic baselines. The PI has previously used off-the-shelf Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs), Network for Greening the Financial System (NGFS) scenarios, and probabilistic Resources for the Future Socioeconomic Projections (RFF-SPs) in past work. However, stakeholder engagement under a current project suggests that policymakers are more interested in "most likely" and "worst-case" scenarios.
> 
> - **Country-Specific Decisions:** 
> 
>   1. Develop risk and adaptation storylines: Domestic and international storylines for each country will provide context and applications for the quantitative risk outcomes and adaptation models (WP2 to WP5 and WP6). These will be developed through workshops. Meetings will also discuss the significance of results in active policy discussions.
> 
>   2. Design interactive visualisations to inform decision-making: Interactive displays will allow stakeholders to explore risks across dimensions of region, time, scenario, and sector, as well as the benefits of adaptation, across adaptation solutions and investment plans. Workshops will be utilised to design and refine interfaces to the project dataset.
